  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "pad_before": 0.25,
      "pad_after": 0.25,
      "min_duration": 3.4,
      "lines": [
        "What happens when a bead chain falls out of a beaker?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obvious",
      "pad_before": 0.15,
      "pad_after": 0.5,
      "min_duration": 3.2,
      "lines": [
        "Obviously, it slides over the rim and drops."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reversal",
      "pad_before": 0.2,
      "pad_after": 0.7,
      "min_duration": 7.0,
      "lines": [
        "But it doesn't. The chain lifts itself out of the beaker and hovers in a loop above the rim, until the beaker is empty. How?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kick",
      "pad_before": 0.2,
      "pad_after": 0.45,
      "min_duration": 6.0,
      "lines": [
        "Each bead is a tiny rigid rod. When the chain yanks one end up, the other end kicks down into the pile. And the pile kicks back."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "equation",
      "pad_before": 0.2,
      "pad_after": 0.45,
      "min_duration": 6.0,
      "lines": [
        "That kick is a fraction, alpha, of the pull. Fountain height is alpha over one minus alpha, times the drop height. Alpha is about one eighth."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scale",
      "pad_before": 0.2,
      "pad_after": 0.5,
      "min_duration": 5.0,
      "lines": [
        "Drop it one and a half meters, and it floats twenty centimeters above the rim. Drop it from a fourth floor balcony, and the fountain is as tall as you."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cta",
      "pad_before": 0.15,
      "pad_after": 0.8,
      "min_duration": 2.6,
      "lines": [
        "Follow for more!"
      ]
    }
  ],
